The SCADA system implements a defence-in-depth security architecture. Communication between the ESP32 edge node and Mosquitto broker, and between Mosquitto and Node-RED, is protected using MQTT over TLS on port 8883. A private project CA is used for broker certificate verification. MQTT username/password authentication and topic-level ACLs provide device authentication and least-privilege authorization. Anonymous MQTT connections and the previous unencrypted MQTT listener are disabled, while Windows Firewall exposes only the required TLS port.

Node-RED communication with the browser is protected using HTTPS. The Node-RED engineering editor has separate administrator authentication, while the SCADA dashboard implements independent Admin and Client accounts. Dashboard sessions are protected using secure cookies and authenticated WebSocket connections. Role-based access control is enforced server-side so that Admin users can acknowledge alarms and modify configuration while Client users have read-only monitoring access.

Together, these controls provide authentication, authorization, confidentiality, server identity verification, least-privilege access, and separation between monitoring and administrative functions.
